    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhen subtracting two fractions with the same denominator, we\n\nContent:\n- subtract their numerators, and\n- keep the denominator the same.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's compute the following difference:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{3}{5} - \\dfrac{2}{5}]\n\nContent:\nWe subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (3)/(5) \u2212 (2)/(5) = (3 \u2212 2)/(5) = (1)/(5).]\n\nContent:\nWe can use a fraction model to confirm this result:\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/e8468c5e2bb1fd0e0000ca0efd3f0d69]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Subtracting Fractions With Like Denominators\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\dfrac 3 7 - \\dfrac 1 7.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (3)/(7) \u2212 (1)/(7) = (3 \u2212 1)/(7) = (2)/(7).]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (5)/(6) \u2212 (4)/(6) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(6)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(12)]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: (5)/(6)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (4)/(5) \u2212 (2)/(5) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (8)/(5)]\nb. [MATH: (8)/(25)]\nc. [MATH: (2)/(0)]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(25)]\ne. [MATH: (2)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Subtracting Fractions With Like Denominators and Simplifying\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\dfrac{5}{6} - \\dfrac{3}{6}?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (5)/(6) \u2212 (3)/(6) = (5 \u2212 3)/(6) = (2)/(6).]\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by [MATH: 2{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{2\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\dfrac{1}{3}]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{5}{6} - \\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac 1 3.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (3)/(4) \u2212 (1)/(4) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(8)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: (3)/(4)]\nd. [MATH: (3)/(8)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (8)/(9) \u2212 (2)/(9) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(9)]\nb. [MATH: (4)/(9)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: (5)/(9)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Converting the Result to a Mixed Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\dfrac{23}{9} - \\dfrac{8}{9}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n[MATH: \\dfrac{23}{9} - \\dfrac{8}{9} = \\dfrac{23 - 8}{9} = \\dfrac{15}{9}.]\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by [MATH: 3{:}]\n[MATH: \\dfrac{15}{9} = \\dfrac{15 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}]\n\nSince [MATH: 5 \\div 3 = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 2,] we can write the answer as a mixed number:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{5}{3} = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: \\dfrac{23}{9} - \\dfrac{8}{9} = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (12)/(5) \u2212 (3)/(5) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2(3)/(5)]\nb. [MATH: 1(4)/(5)]\nc. [MATH: 1(2)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: 2(1)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: 1(3)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (19)/(6) \u2212 (5)/(6) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2(1)/(6)]\nb. [MATH: 1(5)/(6)]\nc. [MATH: 2(2)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: 1(2)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: 2(1)/(3)]"
